# Next.js 14 App Router Project Rules

## TypeScript
- Always use TypeScript with strict type checking
- Define proper interfaces/types for all props, state, and data structures
- Use type inference where appropriate, but be explicit for public APIs
- Avoid `any` type - use `unknown` or proper types instead
- Enable strict mode in tsconfig.json

## Next.js 14 App Router
- Use the App Router (`app/` directory) for all routing
- Use Server Components by default, mark Client Components with `'use client'` directive
- Use `next/link` for navigation instead of `react-router-dom`
- Use `next/navigation` hooks (`useRouter`, `usePathname`) for client-side navigation
- Place shared layouts in `app/layout.tsx`
- Use route groups and parallel routes when appropriate
- Leverage Server Actions for form submissions and mutations
- Use `generateStaticParams` for dynamic routes when possible

## Tailwind CSS
- Follow mobile-first design principles - start with base mobile styles, then add `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:` breakpoints
- Use Tailwind utility classes instead of custom CSS files
- Keep custom CSS minimal - only for complex animations or when Tailwind utilities are insufficient
- Use Tailwind's dark mode classes (`dark:`) for dark mode support
- Ensure high contrast for accessibility (WCAG AA minimum)
- Use semantic color tokens from Tailwind's color palette
- Prefer Tailwind's spacing scale (p-4, m-2, gap-4, etc.)

## Component Structure
- Place all reusable components in `@/components` directory (aliased from `src/components` or `components`)
- Use modular, single-responsibility components
- Export components as named exports when possible
- Use TypeScript interfaces for component props
- Keep components small and focused - extract complex logic into custom hooks or utilities

## Supabase Integration
- All Supabase calls must be handled via Server Actions or a dedicated client utility
- Create a `@/lib/supabase` directory for Supabase client utilities
- Use Server Actions for mutations and data fetching when possible
- Create a client-side Supabase utility for client components that need real-time features
- Never expose Supabase keys in client-side code - use environment variables
- Use TypeScript types generated from Supabase schema when available

## UI/UX Guidelines
- Design with mobile-first approach - ensure all features work on mobile devices
- Keep UI clean, modern, and minimal
- Support Dark Mode using Tailwind's dark mode classes
- Ensure high contrast for text and interactive elements
- Use Lucide React icons consistently throughout the application
- Maintain consistent spacing and typography using Tailwind's design tokens
- Use semantic HTML elements for accessibility

## File Organization
- `app/` - Next.js App Router pages and layouts
- `components/` - Reusable React components
- `lib/` - Utility functions, Supabase clients, helpers
- `types/` - TypeScript type definitions
- `public/` - Static assets
- `styles/` - Global styles (minimal, prefer Tailwind)

## Code Quality
- Use ESLint and Prettier for code formatting
- Follow Next.js and React best practices
- Write self-documenting code with clear variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions and components
- Handle errors gracefully with proper error boundaries and try-catch blocks

## Performance
- Use Next.js Image component for images
- Implement proper loading states and Suspense boundaries
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting when appropriate
- Optimize bundle size by avoiding unnecessary dependencies
- Leverage Next.js caching strategies (revalidate, cache tags)

## Accessibility
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Ensure proper ARIA labels where needed
- Maintain keyboard navigation support
- Ensure color contrast meets WCAG AA standards
- Test with screen readers when possible

